>
> "Like any sumti, "ke'a" can be omitted. The usual presumption in that case
> is that it then falls into the x1 place:" Example 1.7 on page 170.
>

In {tu poi le mlatu pu lacpu cu ratcu} the first place of {lacpu} is
already filled. It is filled with {le mlatu}. Thus we search somewhere else
where {ke'a} can be omitted. It's reasonable to assume that it is omitted
from the second place.

In fact in most cases the first unfilled place is where you put omitted
{ke'a}.

